    "content": "{\"id\": \"e2e13a73-a798-4106-ae77-e70a2fb9c197\", \"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": [{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"No \\u2014 your EBS volumes are not currently protected by AWS Backup in us-east-1.\\n\\nHere's what I found:\\n\\n- **2 EBS volumes** exist in us-east-1: `vol-0c52b1cc0c75c90db` and `vol-0332aa598c22531b4` (both 8 GB, in-use, untagged)\\n- **AWS Backup's protected resources list** shows **zero** EBS resources currently protected\\n- There's a backup plan called **\\\"ec2backup\\\"** with a selection named \\\"EC2-instance\\\" \\u2014 but that selection targets EC2 **instances**, not EBS volumes directly. Since EC2-instance backups create AMIs/snapshots at the instance level rather than registering the volumes as independently protected resources, they wouldn't show up as protected EBS resources either \\u2014 and depending on how that selection is scoped (by resource ARN vs. tag), it may or may not actually be capturing these two volumes.\\n\\nI'd recommend checking whether that \\\"EC2-instance\\\" selection actually includes the instances these two volumes are attached to. Want me to look into which instances these volumes belong to and whether they're covered by that selection?\"}]}",
